Absolutely. Here’s the session log content for the **Azure Load Balancer lab**, capturing what we actually built, tested, learned, and verified.

# Session Log — Azure Load Balancer Lab

## Session Objective

Build and validate an Azure Standard Public Load Balancer using two Ubuntu VMs as backend servers.

The lab was designed to understand:

- Azure Load Balancer architecture
- Frontend public IP
- Backend address pools
- Health probes
- Load-balancing rules
- NSG requirements
- Backend health and failure detection
- Traffic distribution
- Backend recovery

---

## 1. Lab Resource Group

Created the resource group:

```text
Resource Group: az-lb-lab-rg
Location: centralindia
Tags:
  owner=tarun
  AutoDelete=Yes
```

The previous NSG lab resource group was deleted before starting this lab.

---

## 2. Virtual Network

Created:

```text
VNet: az-lb-lab-vnet
Address Space: 10.10.0.0/16
```

Subnets:

```text
az-lb-lab-subnet1 → 10.10.1.0/24
az-lb-lab-subnet2 → 10.10.2.0/24
```

The two backend VMs were intentionally placed in different subnets while remaining inside the same VNet.

---

## 3. Backend VMs

Created two Ubuntu 22.04 VMs.

### VM1

```text
Name: az-lb-lab-vm1
Private IP: 10.10.1.4
Subnet: az-lb-lab-subnet1
```

### VM2

```text
Name: az-lb-lab-vm2
Private IP: 10.10.2.4
Subnet: az-lb-lab-subnet2
```

NICs:

```text
az-lb-lab-vm1VMNic → 10.10.1.4
az-lb-lab-vm2VMNic → 10.10.2.4
```

---

## 4. Nginx Backend Services

Installed Nginx on both VMs.

Configured different responses so that traffic distribution could be observed:

```text
VM1 → Hello from VM1
VM2 → Hello from VM2
```

Verified directly from inside the VNet:

```text
VM1 → VM2
curl http://10.10.2.4
→ Hello from VM2

VM2 → VM1
curl http://10.10.1.4
→ Hello from VM1
```

This confirmed that both backend applications were reachable over HTTP.

---

## 5. NSG Configuration

Both VM NICs had NSGs attached.

Initially, each VM had:

```text
default-allow-ssh
Priority: 1000
Direction: Inbound
Access: Allow
Protocol: TCP
Port: 22
```

Added HTTP access:

```text
allow-http
Priority: 200
Direction: Inbound
Access: Allow
Protocol: TCP
Source: *
Destination: *
Port: 80
```

This was necessary because the Standard Load Balancer requires the backend to allow the relevant inbound traffic.

---

## 6. Public IP

Created:

```text
Public IP: az-lb-lab-pip
IP Address: 20.207.206.109
SKU: Standard
Allocation: Static
Location: centralindia
```

This public IP became the frontend entry point for the Load Balancer.

---

## 7. Azure Load Balancer

Created:

```text
Load Balancer: az-lb-lab-lb
SKU: Standard
Location: centralindia
```

Frontend:

```text
Frontend name: az-lb-lab-frontend
Public IP: az-lb-lab-pip
```

Backend pool automatically created:

```text
az-lb-lab-lbbepool
```

Added:

```text
vm1 → 10.10.1.4
vm2 → 10.10.2.4
```

Verified backend pool membership:

```text
Name    IP
vm1     10.10.1.4
vm2     10.10.2.4
```

---

## 8. Health Probe

Created an HTTP health probe:

```text
Name: az-lb-lab-http-probe
Protocol: HTTP
Port: 80
Path: /
Interval: 15 seconds
```

The probe was associated with the load-balancing rule.

Important concept:

The backend pool contains the VMs, but the health probe determines whether a backend is considered healthy and eligible to receive traffic.

---

## 9. Load-Balancing Rule

Created:

```text
Rule: az-lb-lab-http-rule
Protocol: TCP

Frontend:
  Port 80

Backend:
  Port 80

Backend Pool:
  az-lb-lab-lbbepool

Probe:
  az-lb-lab-http-probe
```

Architecture:

```text
Client
  |
  | TCP :80
  v
20.207.206.109
  |
  v
Azure Load Balancer
  |
  +------ VM1 10.10.1.4 :80
  |
  +------ VM2 10.10.2.4 :80
```

---

## 10. Initial Public Load Balancer Test

Initial request to the public IP failed:

```text
curl.exe http://20.207.206.109
```

The connection timed out.

Troubleshooting showed that Nginx was running on VM1:

```text
LISTEN 0 511 0.0.0.0:80
```

The VM NSGs, however, only allowed SSH at that point.

Added the `allow-http` NSG rule to both VM NSGs.

After that, the Load Balancer became reachable.

---

## 11. Traffic Distribution Test

Executed:

```powershell
1..10 | ForEach-Object {
    curl.exe -s http://20.207.206.109
}
```

Observed:

```text
Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
```

This confirmed that the Load Balancer was distributing traffic across both backend VMs.

Important observation:

Azure Load Balancer should not be thought of as a simplistic round-robin mechanism. Its default load distribution is flow-based, using a hash of connection characteristics.

---

## 12. Backend Failure Experiment

To demonstrate health-probe behavior, Nginx was deliberately stopped on VM1:

```bash
sudo systemctl stop nginx
```

VM1 was still present in the backend pool, but its HTTP service was no longer responding.

Repeated requests to the public Load Balancer IP produced:

```text
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
```

This demonstrated that the Load Balancer detected VM1 as unhealthy through the HTTP health probe and stopped sending new traffic to it.

VM2 continued serving traffic normally.

---

## 13. Backend Recovery

Started Nginx again on VM1:

```bash
sudo systemctl start nginx
```

Verified locally:

```bash
curl http://localhost
```

Result:

```text
Hello from VM1
```

This confirmed that the backend application had recovered.

After the health probe detected the recovered service, VM1 became eligible to receive Load Balancer traffic again.

---

## 14. Important Concepts Learned

### Backend pool membership ≠ backend health

A VM can remain registered in the backend pool while being temporarily excluded from traffic because its health probe is failing.

### Health probes control backend eligibility

The health probe continuously checks:

```text
VM :80 /
```

If the probe fails, the backend is considered unhealthy.

### NSGs still matter

The Load Balancer does not bypass the VM's network security controls.

The backend NSG must permit the required traffic.

### Load Balancer and Nginx have different responsibilities

```text
Azure Load Balancer
    ↓
Receives client traffic
    ↓
Selects healthy backend
    ↓
VM
    ↓
Nginx
    ↓
HTTP response
```

### Failure handling is automatic

We did not manually remove VM1 from the backend pool when Nginx stopped.

The sequence was:

```text
VM1 healthy
    ↓
receives traffic

Nginx stopped
    ↓
health probe fails
    ↓
VM1 excluded from new traffic

Nginx restarted
    ↓
health probe succeeds
    ↓
VM1 eligible again
```

---

## 15. Final Architecture

```text
                         Internet
                            |
                            | HTTP :80
                            v
                  +---------------------+
                  | Static Public IP    |
                  | 20.207.206.109      |
                  +----------+----------+
                             |
                             v
                  +---------------------+
                  | Azure Load Balancer |
                  | Standard            |
                  +----------+----------+
                             |
                    Backend Pool
                  /                     \
                 /                       \
                v                         v
       +----------------+        +----------------+
       | VM1            |        | VM2            |
       | 10.10.1.4      |        | 10.10.2.4      |
       | subnet1        |        | subnet2        |
       | Nginx          |        | Nginx          |
       +----------------+        +----------------+
          Hello VM1                 Hello VM2

                 Health Probe
                  HTTP :80 /
```

